 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Chunked Bounding Box Computation and Normalization](17031-chunked-bounding-box-computation-and-normalization.md)
-Preprocesses splats by dividing them into fixed-size chunks, computing per-chunk bounding boxes, and normalizing data to $[0, 1]$ for efficient storage in 8 separate RGBA32F textures.
+This method divides splats into fixed-size chunks, computes per-chunk bounding boxes for position, scale, color, and SH coefficients, and normalizes the data to $[0, 1]$ for efficient storage. It sets itself apart from siblings by introducing spatial chunking and normalization rather than raw texture packing. Choose it when memory bandwidth is constrained and quantization is acceptable.
 
 ### [Gaussian Splat Data Packing and Compression](17062-gaussian-splat-data-packing-and-compression.md)
-Converts raw splat data into a compressed, GPU-ready byte buffer layout using Morton ordering and aggressive quantization (Norm11/16/8) for real-time rendering.
+This method converts raw splat data into compressed GPU-ready byte buffers using Morton space-filling curve reordering and aggressive per-attribute quantization. It sets itself apart by integrating cache-coherent ordering with chunk-based SH storage. Choose it when VRAM is limited and runtime compression is required.
 
